          "worked": "The radicand requires $169-x^2\\ge0$. Find its boundary values:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}169-x^2&=0\\\\(13-x)(13+x)&=0\\\\x&=-13\\text{ or }x=13\\end{align*}$$\n\nSketch $y=169-x^2$. The downward parabola is on or above the $x$-axis for $-13\\le x\\le13$.\n\nThe full denominator must also satisfy $\\sqrt{169-x^2}-5\\ne0$. Its zeros occur when:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sqrt{169-x^2}&=5\\\\169-x^2&=25\\\\x^2&=144\\\\x&=-12\\text{ or }x=12\\end{align*}$$\n\nExclude $x=-12$ and $x=12$. At $x=\\pm13$, the denominator is $-5$, so both endpoints remain included.\n\nThe domain is $[-13,-12)\\cup(-12,12)\\cup(12,13]$.",
